introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do classes and objects help you build software that scales?"
  overview: "This unit introduces object-oriented programming as a way to organize code around <strong>objects</strong> that combine data and behavior. You will move from writing isolated procedures to designing reusable <strong>classes</strong> with clear responsibilities."
  journey: "You will evaluate the strengths and limits of OOP, design class structures with <strong>UML</strong>, distinguish <strong>static</strong> and <strong>instance</strong> members, construct class code with <code>__init__</code> and <code>self</code>, and apply <strong>encapsulation</strong> to protect data integrity."

concepts:
  - title: "Why OOP is used"
    icon: "fa-cubes"
    content: |
      <p>OOP helps large programs stay manageable by splitting responsibility across classes.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Modularity:</strong> each class has a focused role.</li>
          <li><strong>Reusability:</strong> once a class is written, it can be used in other projects.</li>
          <li><strong>Encapsulation:</strong> class data is updated through controlled methods.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Class design with UML"
    icon: "fa-diagram-project"
    content: |
      <p>Before coding, you design classes using UML class diagrams.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>Top row: class name</li>
          <li>Middle row: attributes and data types</li>
          <li>Bottom row: methods and signatures</li>
          <li><code>+</code> public, <code>-</code> private visibility</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Static vs instance members"
    icon: "fa-sitemap"
    content: |
      <p>Some values belong to every object; others belong to one specific object.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Instance:</strong> unique per object, e.g. <code>self.balance</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Static:</strong> shared by all objects, e.g. <code>next_id</code>.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Constructing safe classes"
    icon: "fa-shield-halved"
    content: |
      <p>You will construct class code and protect data through encapsulation.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>Define classes with constructors (<code>__init__</code>).</li>
          <li>Create objects through instantiation.</li>
          <li>Use getters/setters to validate updates.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Class"
    definition: "A blueprint that defines attributes and methods for a type of object."
  - term: "Object (Instance)"
    definition: "A concrete item created from a class at runtime."
  - term: "Constructor (__init__)"
    definition: "A method that runs during object creation to initialize state."
  - term: "Attribute"
    definition: "Data stored in an object or class."
  - term: "Method"
    definition: "A function that belongs to a class."
  - term: "UML Class Diagram"
    definition: "A three-row diagram showing class name, attributes, and methods."
  - term: "Static Variable"
    definition: "Data shared by all instances of a class."
  - term: "Instance Variable"
    definition: "Data unique to one specific object."
  - term: "Encapsulation"
    definition: "Protecting internal data by exposing controlled methods for access and updates."
  - term: "Accessor / Mutator"
    definition: "Getter and setter methods used to read and validate updates to internal state."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Class and object are the same"
    correction: "A class is the template. An object is one specific instance created from that template."
  - mistake: "Double underscore means true security"
    correction: "In Python, <code>__name</code> uses name mangling to reduce accidental access, but it is not encryption."
  - mistake: "Static values should store per-object data"
    correction: "Static members are shared by all objects. Per-object values belong in instance attributes."

